ML_PAGE("t2-prep", "<header class=\"page-header theme-l2\"><span class=\"eyebrow\">TOPIC 2 · MCQ PREPARATION</span><h2>Topic 2 MCQ Prep</h2><p>The complete MCQ preparation notes in their original order: 35 multiple-choice questions sit inside the sections they belong to, with answers hidden until you reveal them.</p></header><div class=\"study-grid three-col\"><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>35</strong><span>MCQs in the notes</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>34</strong><span>With worked reasoning</span></div><div class=\"metric-card\"><strong>30</strong><span>Difficult-mode questions</span></div></div><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn bg-l2\" data-quiz=\"2\">Start Topic 2 simulator</button></div><details class=\"topic-index toc-panel theme-l2\" id=\"t2p-toc\"><summary><strong>MCQ notes</strong><span class=\"toc-count\">48 sections</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><input type=\"search\" class=\"toc-search\" placeholder=\"Filter sections...\" aria-label=\"Filter sections\"><ul class=\"toc-list\"><li class=\"toc-group\">PART A - STRUCTURES &amp; OBJECTIVES</li><li data-full=\"Part 1 - The Topic 2 MCQ decision tree\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s2\" title=\"Part 1 - The Topic 2 MCQ decision tree\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P1</span>The Topic 2 MCQ decision tree</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 2 - Business structures: the high-yield MCQ distinctions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s3\" title=\"Part 2 - Business structures: the high-yield MCQ distinctions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P2</span>Business structures</button></li><li data-full=\"3. Sole proprietorship\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s4\" title=\"3. Sole proprietorship\"><span class=\"toc-no\">3</span>Sole proprietorship</button></li><li data-full=\"4. Partnership\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s5\" title=\"4. Partnership\"><span class=\"toc-no\">4</span>Partnership</button></li><li data-full=\"5. Sdn. Bhd.\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s6\" title=\"5. Sdn. Bhd.\"><span class=\"toc-no\">5</span>Sdn. Bhd.</button></li><li data-full=\"6. Public Limited Company - Berhad\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s7\" title=\"6. Public Limited Company - Berhad\"><span class=\"toc-no\">6</span>Public Limited Company - Berhad</button></li><li data-full=\"7. LLP\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s8\" title=\"7. LLP\"><span class=\"toc-no\">7</span>LLP</button></li><li data-full=\"8. Corporation: ownership is separated from the corporation itself\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s9\" title=\"8. Corporation: ownership is separated from the corporation itself\"><span class=\"toc-no\">8</span>Corporation: ownership vs entity</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 3 - Corporate objectives\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s10\" title=\"Part 3 - Corporate objectives\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P3</span>Corporate objectives</button></li><li data-full=\"9. Shareholder wealth vs profit\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s11\" title=\"9. Shareholder wealth vs profit\"><span class=\"toc-no\">9</span>Shareholder wealth vs profit</button></li><li data-full=\"10. Triple Bottom Line\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s12\" title=\"10. Triple Bottom Line\"><span class=\"toc-no\">10</span>Triple Bottom Line</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART B - RISK LEVELS &amp; RISK MANAGEMENT</li><li data-full=\"Part 4 - Levels of enterprise risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s14\" title=\"Part 4 - Levels of enterprise risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P4</span>Levels of enterprise risk</button></li><li data-full=\"11. Strategic risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s15\" title=\"11. Strategic risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">11</span>Strategic risk</button></li><li data-full=\"12. Tactical risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s16\" title=\"12. Tactical risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">12</span>Tactical risk</button></li><li data-full=\"13. Operational risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s17\" title=\"13. Operational risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">13</span>Operational risk</button></li><li data-full=\"14. Compliance risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s18\" title=\"14. Compliance risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">14</span>Compliance risk</button></li><li data-full=\"15. Strategic vs operational - common trap\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s19\" title=\"15. Strategic vs operational - common trap\"><span class=\"toc-no\">15</span>Strategic vs operational - common trap</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 5 - What is risk management?\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s20\" title=\"Part 5 - What is risk management?\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P5</span>What is risk management?</button></li><li data-full=\"16. Risk management should be systematic\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s21\" title=\"16. Risk management should be systematic\"><span class=\"toc-no\">16</span>Risk management should be systematic</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 6 - Pre-loss vs post-loss objectives\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s22\" title=\"Part 6 - Pre-loss vs post-loss objectives\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P6</span>Pre-loss vs post-loss objectives</button></li><li data-full=\"17. Pre-loss vs post-loss MCQ shortcut\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s23\" title=\"17. Pre-loss vs post-loss MCQ shortcut\"><span class=\"toc-no\">17</span>Pre-loss vs post-loss MCQ shortcut</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART C - EVOLUTION TO ERM</li><li data-full=\"Part 7 - Evolution of risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s25\" title=\"Part 7 - Evolution of risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P7</span>Evolution of risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"18. Early/traditional risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s26\" title=\"18. Early/traditional risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">18</span>Early/traditional risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"19. The silo approach\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s27\" title=\"19. The silo approach\"><span class=\"toc-no\">19</span>The silo approach</button></li><li data-full=\"20. Why silos are dangerous\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s28\" title=\"20. Why silos are dangerous\"><span class=\"toc-no\">20</span>Why silos are dangerous</button></li><li data-full=\"21. Conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM)\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s29\" title=\"21. Conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM)\"><span class=\"toc-no\">21</span>Conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM)</button></li><li data-full=\"22. Integrated Risk Management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s30\" title=\"22. Integrated Risk Management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">22</span>Integrated Risk Management</button></li><li data-full=\"23. Enterprise Risk Management - ERM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s31\" title=\"23. Enterprise Risk Management - ERM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">23</span>Enterprise Risk Management - ERM</button></li><li data-full=\"24. ERM vs traditional RM\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s32\" title=\"24. ERM vs traditional RM\"><span class=\"toc-no\">24</span>ERM vs traditional RM</button></li><li data-full=\"25. ERM is not about eliminating all risk\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s33\" title=\"25. ERM is not about eliminating all risk\"><span class=\"toc-no\">25</span>ERM is not about eliminating all risk</button></li><li data-full=\"26. ERM and value creation\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s34\" title=\"26. ERM and value creation\"><span class=\"toc-no\">26</span>ERM and value creation</button></li><li data-full=\"27. New orientation of corporate risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s35\" title=\"27. New orientation of corporate risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">27</span>New orientation of corporate risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"28. Holistic Risk Management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s36\" title=\"28. Holistic Risk Management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">28</span>Holistic Risk Management</button></li><li data-full=\"29. Emerging risks drove the evolution\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s37\" title=\"29. Emerging risks drove the evolution\"><span class=\"toc-no\">29</span>Emerging risks drove the evolution</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 8 - Technology and analytics\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s38\" title=\"Part 8 - Technology and analytics\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P8</span>Technology and analytics</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 9 - High-yield MCQ trap table\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s39\" title=\"Part 9 - High-yield MCQ trap table\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P9</span>High-yield MCQ trap table</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART D - MOCK MCQs &amp; ANSWERS</li><li data-full=\"Part 10 - Mock MCQ Set 1: business structure\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s41\" title=\"Part 10 - Mock MCQ Set 1: business structure\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P10</span>Mock MCQ Set 1: business structure</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 11 - Goals and risk levels\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s42\" title=\"Part 11 - Goals and risk levels\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P11</span>Goals and risk levels</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 12 - Risk management process\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s43\" title=\"Part 12 - Risk management process\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P12</span>Risk management process</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 13 - Evolution of risk management\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s44\" title=\"Part 13 - Evolution of risk management\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P13</span>Evolution of risk management</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 14 - ERM questions\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s45\" title=\"Part 14 - ERM questions\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P14</span>ERM questions</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 15 - Harder analysis-level MCQs\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s46\" title=\"Part 15 - Harder analysis-level MCQs\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P15</span>Harder analysis-level MCQs</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 16 - Answers and reasoning\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s47\" title=\"Part 16 - Answers and reasoning\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P16</span>Answers and reasoning</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART E - TRICKY QUESTIONS</li><li data-full=\"Part 17 - Very tricky MCQs where two answers seem right\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s49\" title=\"Part 17 - Very tricky MCQs where two answers seem right\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P17</span>Tricky \"two answers\" MCQs</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 18 - Questions based on wording traps\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s50\" title=\"Part 18 - Questions based on wording traps\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P18</span>Questions based on wording traps</button></li><li class=\"toc-group\">PART F - FINAL REVIEW</li><li data-full=\"Part 19 - The evolution you should be able to reconstruct from memory\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s52\" title=\"Part 19 - The evolution you should be able to reconstruct from memory\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P19</span>Reconstruct the evolution</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 20 - What I would memorise the night before\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s53\" title=\"Part 20 - What I would memorise the night before\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P20</span>What I would memorise the night before</button></li><li data-full=\"Part 21 - The single most important MCQ trick for Topic 2\"><button type=\"button\" data-toc=\"t2p-s54\" title=\"Part 21 - The single most important MCQ trick for Topic 2\"><span class=\"toc-no\">P21</span>The key MCQ trick</button></li></ul></details><details class=\"prep-notes theme-l2\" open><summary><div><strong>Topic 2 MCQ preparation</strong><small>Distinctions, traps and practice questions, section by section, answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"markdown-body compact\"><div class=\"notes-toolbar\"><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"open\">Expand all</button><button type=\"button\" data-sections=\"close\">Collapse all</button><span>48 sections · click a heading to open it</span></div><p>For <strong>Topic 2</strong>, MCQs are likely to test whether you can distinguish <strong>business structures, levels of risk, purposes of risk management, and especially the evolution from traditional/silo risk management to ERM</strong>.</p>\n<p>The trap is that several choices may sound sensible. You need to identify <strong>what dimension the question is actually asking about</strong>.</p>\n<p>Topic 2 covers business types and associated risks, origins of risk management, principles/aims, and the evolution of corporate risk management.</p>\n<p>Use this mental sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Business Structure → Objectives → Risk Level → Risk Management → Traditional RM vs ERM\"><span>Business Structure</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Objectives</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Level</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Risk Management</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Traditional RM vs ERM</span></div><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t2p-d1\">PART A - STRUCTURES &amp; OBJECTIVES</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s2\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 1 - The Topic 2 MCQ decision tree</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>When you see a question, ask:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{1.\\ Is this about legal structure/liability?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{2.\\ Is this about the company's objective?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{3.\\ Is this strategic, tactical, operational or compliance risk?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{4.\\ Is this pre-loss or post-loss risk management?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{5.\\ Is this traditional/silo RM or ERM?}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{6.\\ Is the firm merely reducing losses, or using risk information to improve decisions and create value?}$$</div>\n<p>That last distinction is particularly important.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s3\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 2 - Business structures: the high-yield MCQ distinctions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_structures\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Legal form moves the boundary of who absorbs the loss; it does not remove business risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides identify five main Malaysian business entities:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Sole Proprietorship, Partnership, LLP, Sdn. Bhd., Bhd.</span></div>\n<p>You should not just memorise names. Think:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Who owns it, who manages it, and who is liable if it fails?</strong></p>\n</blockquote></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s4\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">3. Sole proprietorship</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_liability_sim\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Where does the loss stop?</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Pick a legal form and the size of the debts; see who pays: the business, the owners personally, or the creditors.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides state:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Unlimited liability</span></div>\n<p>The owner is personally liable for business debts and obligations.</p>\n<h4>MCQ shortcut</h4>\n<p>If the question says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The owner may have to use personal assets to settle business debts\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Sole proprietorship / unlimited liability</span></div>\n<p>Other characteristics shown in the slides include:</p>\n<p>single ownership,</p>\n<p>one-person capital,</p>\n<p>one-person control,</p>\n<p>fewer formalities.</p>\n<h4>Main MCQ trap</h4>\n<p>Easy to start ≠ low risk.</p>\n<p>A sole proprietorship may be simple administratively while exposing the owner personally.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s5\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">4. Partnership</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides also describe partnership liability as generally unlimited, with partners potentially jointly and severally liable for partnership debts.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"More owners → More resources/expertise\"><span>More owners</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>More resources/expertise</span></div>\n<p>but also:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"More people → coordination + liability + conflict risk\"><span>More people</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>coordination + liability + conflict risk</span></div>\n<h4>MCQ trap</h4>\n<p>A partnership does <strong>not</strong> automatically provide limited liability merely because multiple owners share the business.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s6\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">5. Sdn. Bhd.</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides state that shareholders generally have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Limited liability</span></div>\n<p>and that a Sdn. Bhd. is a separate legal entity from its shareholders.</p>\n<p>So if the company owes:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$RM5m$$</div>\n<p>a shareholder who invested RM100,000 is generally exposed only to that investment rather than automatically owing the RM5m personally.</p>\n<h4>MCQ shortcut</h4>\n<p>\"Separate legal entity\" + \"shareholders generally liable only for investment\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{Sdn. Bhd. / company}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s7\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">6. Public Limited Company - Berhad</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe Bhd. as having limited shareholder liability and associate it with larger companies listed on the stock exchange.</p>\n<p>If a question contrasts:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Sdn. Bhd.}$$</div>\n<p>with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Bhd.}$$</div>\n<p>look for clues about:</p>\n<p>public company,</p>\n<p>stock exchange,</p>\n<p>larger shareholder base.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s8\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">7. LLP</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe LLPs as combining:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Partnership simplicity + company-like legal protection</span></div>\n<p>with partners generally liable only for their investment.</p>\n<h4>MCQ shortcut</h4>\n<p>\"Professional firm + limited liability + partnership flexibility\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{LLP}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s9\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">8. Corporation: ownership is separated from the corporation itself</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_ownership_control\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Limited liability and professional management solve concentration risk but open an agency gap.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The corporation is responsible for its own obligations, while owners are not generally liable for corporate obligations.</p>\n<p>The deeper distinction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\\text{Ownership} \\neq \\text{Management}}$$</div>\n<p>Shareholders elect the board; management runs the company.</p>\n<h4>Likely MCQ</h4>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which structure creates a clear separation between ownership and management?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Corporation</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s10\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 3 - Corporate objectives</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_objective_tensions\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Dashed red lines mark goals that conflict; which one dominates decides what counts as risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter lists goals such as:</p>\n<p>survival,</p>\n<p>beating competition,</p>\n<p>maximising sales,</p>\n<p>maximising profit,</p>\n<p>minimising costs,</p>\n<p>maximising market share,</p>\n<p>maximising shareholder wealth.</p>\n<p>MCQs may exploit the fact that these objectives can conflict.</p>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Minimise costs}$$</div>\n<p>may conflict with:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{quality or long-term growth}$$</div>\n<p>Likewise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Maximise sales}$$</div>\n<p>does not necessarily mean:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Maximise profit}$$</div>\n<p>because price cuts can increase sales but reduce margins.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s11\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">9. Shareholder wealth vs profit</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Do not automatically treat these as identical.</p>\n<p>A company can increase short-term profit by cutting maintenance.</p>\n<p>But that may increase:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{future operational risk}$$</div>\n<p>and reduce long-term value.</p>\n<p>For MCQ logic:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Short-term profit} \\neq \\text{necessarily long-term shareholder wealth}\n}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s12\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">10. Triple Bottom Line</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides move from the single financial bottom line toward:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>People + Planet + Profit</span></div>\n<p>This links financial performance with social and environmental considerations.</p>\n<p>If the question mentions:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>economic, social and environmental performance</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Triple Bottom Line</span></div></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t2p-d13\">PART B - RISK LEVELS &amp; RISK MANAGEMENT</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s14\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 4 - Levels of enterprise risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_risk_levels\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk viewed at four levels, from direction-setting down to the rules the firm must follow.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Page 23 separates risk into:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Tactical</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance</span></div>\n<p>and says these levels help facilitate risk management and aggregation.</p>\n<p>This is prime MCQ material.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s15\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">11. Strategic risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Are we choosing the right direction?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>entering a new market,</p>\n<p>acquisition,</p>\n<p>major product shift,</p>\n<p>changing business model.</p>\n<h4>Shortcut</h4>\n<p>Long-term corporate direction:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic risk</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s16\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">12. Tactical risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>How are we going to execute the strategy?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The slides place examples such as:</p>\n<p>credit,</p>\n<p>market,</p>\n<p>IT,</p>\n<p>marketing,</p>\n<p>competitive risk</p>\n<p>under tactical risk.</p>\n<h4>Example</h4>\n<p>Objective:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>expand internationally.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Decision:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>how to finance the expansion and price the product?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Tactical</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s17\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">13. Operational risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Can daily operations execute the plan?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>machine failure,</p>\n<p>system outage,</p>\n<p>staff error,</p>\n<p>process failure.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>day-to-day execution</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s18\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">14. Compliance risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Are we following the law/regulations?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Examples:</p>\n<p>licensing,</p>\n<p>safety regulation,</p>\n<p>reporting rules,</p>\n<p>legal standards.</p>\n<p>If the problem arises because:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"The firm violated regulation X\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance risk</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s19\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">15. Strategic vs operational - common trap</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_level_sorter\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Strategic, tactical, operational or compliance?</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Decide which level each risk belongs to, then reveal.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Company decides to enter Thailand:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic</span></div>\n<p>Factory machine in Thailand breaks:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational</span></div>\n<p>Same project. Different level.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s20\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 5 - What is risk management?</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_rm_cycle\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risk management loops: monitoring feeds back into identification as risks change.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter defines risk management as identifying, assessing and controlling threats to organisational capital and earnings.</p>\n<p>Another slide describes it as a systematic process of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Identify → Analyse → Treat → Monitor\"><span>Identify</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Analyse</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Treat</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Monitor</span></div>\n<p>and emphasises improved decision-making, opportunity identification and better use of resources.</p>\n<h4>Important MCQ point</h4>\n<p>Risk management is <strong>not simply insurance</strong>.</p>\n<p>And it is not simply:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>reduce every risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>It supports:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>better decisions under uncertainty</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s21\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">16. Risk management should be systematic</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>If an option says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>ad hoc response after problems occur</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>that is generally weaker than:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>systematic identification, assessment, treatment and monitoring.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>The chapter consistently favours:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>logical + systematic + continuous</span></div>\n<p>risk management.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s22\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 6 - Pre-loss vs post-loss objectives</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_pre_post_loss\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Pre-loss objectives prepare; post-loss objectives keep the firm alive and growing after the event.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is highly testable.</p>\n<p>The slides explicitly separate them.</p>\n<h4>Pre-loss</h4>\n<p>Before the event:</p>\n<p>prepare economically,</p>\n<p>reduce anxiety,</p>\n<p>meet legal obligations.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Preparation</span></div>\n<h4>Post-loss</h4>\n<p>After the event:</p>\n<p>survival,</p>\n<p>continuous operations,</p>\n<p>stabilise earnings,</p>\n<p>maintain growth,</p>\n<p>reduce effects on people/society.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Recovery + resilience</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s23\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">17. Pre-loss vs post-loss MCQ shortcut</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Buying insurance:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Pre-loss</span></div>\n<p>Planning emergency response:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Pre-loss</span></div>\n<p>Keeping company alive after a disaster:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Post-loss</span></div>\n<p>Restoring operations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Post-loss</span></div></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t2p-d24\">PART C - EVOLUTION TO ERM</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s25\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 7 - Evolution of risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_rm_evolution\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Risks accumulated rather than disappeared, so the management approach had to widen.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is probably the most important MCQ section in Topic 2.</p>\n<p>The slides explain that risk management historically began as a narrow <strong>insurance-based discipline</strong>, expanded into financial risk management, and eventually evolved into broader, holistic enterprise risk management.</p>\n<p>Use this sequence:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Insurance → Traditional/Silo RM → Integrated RM → ERM → Holistic RM\"><span>Insurance</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Traditional/Silo RM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Integrated RM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>ERM</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Holistic RM</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s26\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">18. Early/traditional risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Originally:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Pure risks + insurance</span></div>\n<p>Typical risks:</p>\n<p>fire,</p>\n<p>flood,</p>\n<p>physical hazards.</p>\n<p>Main mindset:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Loss occurs → insurance compensates\"><span>Loss occurs</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>insurance compensates</span></div>\n<h4>MCQ clue</h4>\n<p>\"Narrow\", \"insurance-based\", \"physical hazard\", \"reactive\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{Traditional risk management}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s27\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">19. The silo approach</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The slides describe traditional risk management as managing risks in silos and focusing particularly on physical hazards and financial risks.</p>\n<p>Silo means:</p>\n<p>Finance handles finance risk.</p>\n<p>IT handles IT risk.</p>\n<p>Operations handles operational risk.</p>\n<p>Each one analyses risks separately.</p>\n<h4>Main weakness</h4>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk interdependencies may be missed</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s28\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">20. Why silos are dangerous</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Suppose:</p>\n<p>FX risk = low.</p>\n<p>Supplier risk = low.</p>\n<p>Debt risk = low.</p>\n<p>Market risk = low.</p>\n<p>Individually:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$R_i &lt; \\text{limit}$$</div>\n<p>But all depend on the same geopolitical environment.</p>\n<p>A single shock triggers all four.</p>\n<p>Then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Each risk looked acceptable individually, but aggregate risk was high.</span></div>\n<p>That is the ERM problem.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s29\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">21. Conventional Corporate Risk Management (CRM)</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_crm_vs_erm\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Conventional CRM manages financial risks in separate boxes; ERM widens the spectrum and manages it as one.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The slides describe CRM as focusing mainly on financial risks such as:</p>\n<p>credit,</p>\n<p>market,</p>\n<p>liquidity</p>\n<p>and using tools like:</p>\n<p>hedging,</p>\n<p>insurance,</p>\n<p>futures,</p>\n<p>options.</p>\n<p>It often uses the silo approach.</p>\n<h4>MCQ shortcut</h4>\n<p>\"Credit + market + liquidity + hedging + silo\"</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{Conventional CRM}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s30\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">22. Integrated Risk Management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>This stage expands risk management into strategic decision-making.</p>\n<p>Instead of:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Make strategy}$$</div>\n<p>then:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>ask risk department to deal with consequences</span></div>\n<p>the organisation starts integrating risk analysis into planning.</p>\n<p>MCQ clue:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk considerations are incorporated into strategy and decision-making.\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\rightarrow\n\\boxed{\\text{Integrated Risk Management}}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s31\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">23. Enterprise Risk Management - ERM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Your slides define ERM as a <strong>value-based management approach</strong> that provides a holistic framework for measuring and managing corporate risks with the objective of creating long-term firm value.</p>\n<p>The three most important words are:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Holistic</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Integrated</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Value</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s32\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">24. ERM vs traditional RM</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Traditional:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Manage individual risks separately</span></div>\n<p>ERM:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Manage risks collectively relative to enterprise objectives</span></div>\n<p>Traditional:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Risk = something bad}$$</div>\n<p>ERM:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>Risk = uncertainty that must be understood in relation to value</span></div>\n<p>Traditional:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{insurance / hedging}$$</div>\n<p>ERM:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>strategy + operations + finance + reputation + compliance + emerging risks</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s33\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">25. ERM is not about eliminating all risk</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_risk_appetite\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Risk appetite and capacity</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Set risk appetite and capacity; ERM picks the best return that fits, not automatically the safest.</figcaption></figure></div><p>This is a major trap.</p>\n<p>If an MCQ says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The primary purpose of ERM is to eliminate all enterprise risk.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>False</span></div>\n<p>Why?</p>\n<p>Because business itself requires risk.</p>\n<p>ERM aims to:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>understand, manage and optimise risk relative to objectives</span></div>\n<p>not make the organisation incapable of taking any risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s34\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">26. ERM and value creation</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The strongest ERM idea in your slides is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Risk management should support long-term firm value</span></div>\n<p>not simply:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{minimise losses}$$</div>\n<p>Therefore if an MCQ asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Which approach views risk management as part of value creation?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>the best answer is typically:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s35\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">27. New orientation of corporate risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The later slide explicitly contrasts conventional CRM's silo approach with an expanded spectrum of risks managed holistically and integratively.</p>\n<p>Think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{CRM}\n=\n\\text{narrower financial focus}\n}$$</div>\n<p>versus:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ERM}\n=\n\\text{broader enterprise-wide focus}\n}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s36\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">28. Holistic Risk Management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>The chapter's evolution moves further toward integrating risk management with:</p>\n<p>governance,</p>\n<p>sustainability,</p>\n<p>compliance,</p>\n<p>broader organisational functions.</p>\n<p>If an MCQ says:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Risk management integrated with sustainability, governance and compliance\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>think:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Holistic Risk Management</span></div>\n<p>rather than purely traditional RM.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s37\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">29. Emerging risks drove the evolution</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_risk_universe\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Each era added risk categories on top of the old ones.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter says emerging social and environmental risks, changing business conditions and compliance requirements contributed to the need for stronger ERM frameworks.</p>\n<p>So if the question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Why did RM evolve?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Do not choose:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Because insurance stopped existing.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Insurance still exists.</p>\n<p>Better answer:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>The risk universe became broader and more interconnected.</span></div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s38\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 8 - Technology and analytics</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_tech_double_edge\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Analytics improve detection and modelling but bring model, cyber, data and dependency risk.</figcaption></figure></div><p>The chapter states that data analytics, AI and machine learning can improve:</p>\n<p>risk identification,</p>\n<p>assessment,</p>\n<p>monitoring,</p>\n<p>modelling,</p>\n<p>scenario analysis,</p>\n<p>real-time monitoring.</p>\n<p>The point is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Technology improves risk-management capability</span></div>\n<p>but it does not replace governance or judgement.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s39\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 9 - High-yield MCQ trap table</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Question clue</th>\n<th>Best concept</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Owner personally liable</td>\n<td>Sole proprietorship / unlimited liability</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Separate legal entity</td>\n<td>Company/corporation</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Partnership flexibility + limited liability</td>\n<td>LLP</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-term direction</td>\n<td>Strategic risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>How strategy is executed</td>\n<td>Tactical risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day-to-day process failure</td>\n<td>Operational risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Law/regulation</td>\n<td>Compliance risk</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Before loss</td>\n<td>Pre-loss objective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Survival/recovery after loss</td>\n<td>Post-loss objective</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Insurance-based, narrow</td>\n<td>Traditional RM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risks handled separately</td>\n<td>Silo approach</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Credit + market + liquidity focus</td>\n<td>Conventional CRM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Risk included in strategy</td>\n<td>Integrated RM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Enterprise-wide, holistic</td>\n<td>ERM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Long-term value creation</td>\n<td>ERM</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sustainability + governance + compliance integrated</td>\n<td>Holistic RM</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t2p-d40\">PART D - MOCK MCQs &amp; ANSWERS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s41\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 10 - Mock MCQ Set 1: business structure</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Try answering before checking the solutions.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q1</span><div><p>A business owner is personally responsible for all business debts.</p>\n<p>Which structure most clearly fits?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sdn. Bhd.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sole proprietorship</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Bhd.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> LLP</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Sole proprietors have unlimited liability.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q2</span><div><p>Which structure is described as a separate legal entity from its shareholders?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sole proprietorship</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> General partnership</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sdn. Bhd.</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Informal business</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The slides explicitly describe a Sdn. Bhd. as separate from its shareholders.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q3</span><div><p>Which business form combines partnership-style flexibility with limited-liability protection?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sole proprietorship</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> LLP</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> General partnership</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Bhd. only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>LLP combines partnership characteristics with limited-liability protection.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q4</span><div><p>Which statement best describes a corporation?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Owners are always personally responsible for company obligations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The corporation is legally separate from owners</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Ownership and management must always be identical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It cannot have a board</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The corporation is legally distinct from owners and is responsible for its own obligations.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q5</span><div><p>Why can a sole proprietorship represent concentrated risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ownership, management and capital may depend heavily on one person</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It always has too many shareholders</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It has double taxation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It has no liability</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The same individual may provide ownership, management and capital, creating concentration.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s42\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 11 - Goals and risk levels</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q6</span><div><p>A company decides whether to enter the Indonesian market.</p>\n<p>This is primarily:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Operational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Strategic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Compliance risk only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tactical risk only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Entering a market changes the firm's long-term direction.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q7</span><div><p>After deciding to enter Indonesia, management chooses how to finance the expansion.</p>\n<p>This is most closely associated with:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Tactical risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pure risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Operational risk only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compliance risk only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Financing is part of implementing the chosen strategy.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Tactical</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q8</span><div><p>A factory's production system unexpectedly fails.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Strategic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Compliance risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Operational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tactical marketing risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Day-to-day system failure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q9</span><div><p>A company fails to comply with required environmental legislation.</p>\n<p>This is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Compliance risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Strategic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Credit risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tactical pricing risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Violation of laws/regulations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q10</span><div><p>Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Maximising sales always maximises profit</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Minimising costs always increases long-term value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Corporate objectives can conflict</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Shareholder wealth and current-year profit are always identical</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Corporate goals can conflict.</p>\n<p>Higher sales do not guarantee higher profit, for example.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s43\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 12 - Risk management process</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q11</span><div><p>Which sequence most closely reflects risk management in the chapter?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Ignore → insure → recover</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Identify → analyse → treat → monitor</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Invest → borrow → insure → sell</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Monitor → ignore → transfer</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The chapter's logic is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Identify → Analyse → Treat → Monitor</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q12</span><div><p>Which statement best describes risk management?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It is only useful after a loss occurs</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It is mainly a form of insurance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It is a logical and systematic approach supporting decision-making</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Its only objective is to eliminate uncertainty</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Risk management is described as logical and systematic and as improving decision-making.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q13</span><div><p>Buying appropriate insurance before a disaster is primarily associated with:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Post-loss objective</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pre-loss objective</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Strategic failure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compliance failure</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Insurance is arranged before the loss.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Pre-loss</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q14</span><div><p>Ensuring that the organisation survives following a major disaster is a:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pre-loss objective</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Post-loss objective</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Tactical objective only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Purely financial hedge</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Survival after an event is explicitly a post-loss objective.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q15</span><div><p>Restoring business operations following a major loss is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pre-loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Post-loss</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Avoidance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Strategic risk identification</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Continuity and recovery are post-loss objectives.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s44\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 13 - Evolution of risk management</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q16</span><div><p>Historically, risk management was primarily:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ESG-based</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Insurance-based</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> AI-based</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Strategic-value based</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Historically, the discipline was primarily insurance-based.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q17</span><div><p>Traditional risk management mainly concentrated on:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Physical hazards and financial risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sustainability and AI risks only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Strategic opportunities only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Enterprise-wide interdependencies</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Traditional RM focused particularly on physical hazards and financial risks.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q18</span><div><p>Which is the defining weakness of silo risk management?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It cannot insure physical assets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risks may be assessed separately without recognising interactions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It considers too many risks together</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It always overestimates strategic risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The core weakness is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>independent analysis may miss interdependencies</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q19</span><div><p>Conventional corporate risk management mainly focuses on:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Social and environmental risks only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Credit, market and liquidity risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Reputation only</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Employee welfare only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Conventional CRM focuses strongly on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>credit + market + liquidity</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q20</span><div><p>Which tool is particularly associated with conventional financial risk management in the slides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Hedging</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Employee volunteering</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Corporate philanthropy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sustainability reporting</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>The slides mention hedging, including futures and options.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s45\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 14 - ERM questions</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q21</span><div><p>Which statement best describes ERM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Insurance for all company assets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Independent management of each departmental risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> A holistic framework for managing corporate risks to create long-term value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Elimination of all risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>ERM is holistic and value-oriented.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q22</span><div><p>Which feature most clearly distinguishes ERM from silo risk management?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ERM ignores financial risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ERM assesses risks collectively across the enterprise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ERM removes the need for managers</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ERM focuses only on insurance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>ERM considers risks across the enterprise rather than independently.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q23</span><div><p>A company separately manages FX, supply chain, credit and market risk. All four depend heavily on political stability in the same country, but nobody assesses the combined exposure.</p>\n<p>The main problem is:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Too much diversification</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Silo risk management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Excessive ERM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Post-loss management</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This is a textbook silo problem.</p>\n<p>Every department may be technically correct in isolation while the enterprise-level conclusion is wrong.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q24</span><div><p>Which statement is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ERM tries to remove all uncertainty</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ERM supports better decisions about risk and opportunity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ERM only deals with pure risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ERM excludes strategy</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>ERM improves decision-making under uncertainty.</p>\n<p>It does not require zero risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q25</span><div><p>Which approach is most clearly described as \"value-based\"?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pure insurance management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Enterprise Risk Management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sole proprietorship management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Loss prevention only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Your slides explicitly call ERM a:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>value-based management approach</span></div></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s46\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 15 - Harder analysis-level MCQs</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q26</span><div><p>A company has four departments. Each reports that its risk is individually \"low.\" However, all risks could materialise simultaneously if oil prices collapse.</p>\n<p>What has management failed to consider?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Limited liability</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk interdependency and aggregation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Legal ownership</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Post-loss survival</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>They failed to recognise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>common drivers + risk correlation + aggregation</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q27</span><div><p>Management has already approved an overseas acquisition. The risk department is brought in afterward only to insure the new assets.</p>\n<p>Which approach does this most closely resemble?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Fully integrated ERM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Traditional/reactive risk management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Holistic sustainability risk management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Strategic risk integration</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Management decided first and only later transferred some financial consequences.</p>\n<p>That is much closer to traditional/reactive risk management than integrated ERM.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q28</span><div><p>Before approving an acquisition, the board evaluates financial, strategic, operational, reputation and compliance risks together.</p>\n<p>This is most consistent with:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Silo risk management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Enterprise Risk Management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Insurance-only RM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Pure-risk management</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Multiple enterprise risks are being considered collectively before the decision.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q29</span><div><p>Which statement best captures the relationship between risk and corporate objectives?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Risk management operates independently of strategy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk matters because uncertainty may affect organisational objectives</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> All corporate objectives can be maximised simultaneously</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only financial objectives create risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>This links directly back to Topic 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>risk matters because uncertainty affects objectives</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q30</span><div><p>A company rejects every investment containing uncertainty.</p>\n<p>From an ERM perspective, this policy is problematic because:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> All risk must be accepted</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Eliminating risk can also eliminate value-creating opportunities</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> ERM requires maximum risk-taking</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Risk has no relationship to returns</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Business requires risk-taking.</p>\n<p>Eliminating every risky activity could also eliminate:</p>\n<p>innovation,</p>\n<p>growth,</p>\n<p>investment,</p>\n<p>opportunity.</p>\n<p>ERM seeks <strong>appropriate risk</strong>, not zero risk.</p></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s47\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 16 - Answers and reasoning</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Show the answer key</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><h3>Q1 - B</h3>\n<p>Sole proprietors have unlimited liability.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q2 - C</h3>\n<p>The slides explicitly describe a Sdn. Bhd. as separate from its shareholders.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q3 - B</h3>\n<p>LLP combines partnership characteristics with limited-liability protection.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q4 - B</h3>\n<p>The corporation is legally distinct from owners and is responsible for its own obligations.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q5 - A</h3>\n<p>The same individual may provide ownership, management and capital, creating concentration.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q6 - B</h3>\n<p>Entering a market changes the firm's long-term direction.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q7 - A</h3>\n<p>Financing is part of implementing the chosen strategy.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Tactical</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q8 - C</h3>\n<p>Day-to-day system failure:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q9 - A</h3>\n<p>Violation of laws/regulations:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Compliance</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q10 - C</h3>\n<p>Corporate goals can conflict.</p>\n<p>Higher sales do not guarantee higher profit, for example.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q11 - B</h3>\n<p>The chapter's logic is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Identify → Analyse → Treat → Monitor</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q12 - C</h3>\n<p>Risk management is described as logical and systematic and as improving decision-making.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q13 - B</h3>\n<p>Insurance is arranged before the loss.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Pre-loss</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q14 - B</h3>\n<p>Survival after an event is explicitly a post-loss objective.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q15 - B</h3>\n<p>Continuity and recovery are post-loss objectives.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q16 - B</h3>\n<p>Historically, the discipline was primarily insurance-based.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q17 - A</h3>\n<p>Traditional RM focused particularly on physical hazards and financial risks.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q18 - B</h3>\n<p>The core weakness is:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>independent analysis may miss interdependencies</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q19 - B</h3>\n<p>Conventional CRM focuses strongly on:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>credit + market + liquidity</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q20 - A</h3>\n<p>The slides mention hedging, including futures and options.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q21 - C</h3>\n<p>ERM is holistic and value-oriented.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q22 - B</h3>\n<p>ERM considers risks across the enterprise rather than independently.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q23 - B</h3>\n<p>This is a textbook silo problem.</p>\n<p>Every department may be technically correct in isolation while the enterprise-level conclusion is wrong.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q24 - B</h3>\n<p>ERM improves decision-making under uncertainty.</p>\n<p>It does not require zero risk.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q25 - B</h3>\n<p>Your slides explicitly call ERM a:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>value-based management approach</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q26 - B</h3>\n<p>They failed to recognise:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>common drivers + risk correlation + aggregation</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q27 - B</h3>\n<p>Management decided first and only later transferred some financial consequences.</p>\n<p>That is much closer to traditional/reactive risk management than integrated ERM.</p>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q28 - B</h3>\n<p>Multiple enterprise risks are being considered collectively before the decision.</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q29 - B</h3>\n<p>This links directly back to Topic 1:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single\"><span>risk matters because uncertainty affects objectives</span></div>\n<hr />\n<h3>Q30 - B</h3>\n<p>Business requires risk-taking.</p>\n<p>Eliminating every risky activity could also eliminate:</p>\n<p>innovation,</p>\n<p>growth,</p>\n<p>investment,</p>\n<p>opportunity.</p>\n<p>ERM seeks <strong>appropriate risk</strong>, not zero risk.</p>\n<hr /></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t2p-d48\">PART E - TRICKY QUESTIONS</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s49\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 17 - Very tricky MCQs where two answers seem right</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>These are the questions I'd be most careful with.</p>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q31</span><div><p>A company chooses to expand into Vietnam. Later, its accounting software fails and prevents invoices from being processed.</p>\n<p>Which statement is best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both are strategic risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Expansion is strategic; software failure is operational</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Expansion is operational; software failure is strategic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Both are tactical</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The <strong>decision direction</strong> is strategic.</p>\n<p>The <strong>execution failure</strong> is operational.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q32</span><div><p>Management evaluates currency, supplier, reputation and regulatory risks before deciding whether to enter a country.</p>\n<p>What makes this closer to ERM than conventional CRM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It includes insurance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It considers a broad portfolio of risks as part of strategy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It considers currency risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It uses financial information</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Currency risk by itself could easily belong to conventional CRM.</p>\n<p>What makes this ERM is the:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>breadth + integration + strategic connection</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q33</span><div><p>After a major fire, a company wants to continue paying employees and serving customers.</p>\n<p>Which objective is most relevant?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pre-loss legal compliance</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Post-loss continuity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Silo management</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Tactical risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The loss has already happened.</p>\n<p>So the question concerns:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>continuity and survival</span></div></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q34</span><div><p>A firm's risk department perfectly hedges foreign-exchange exposure but management launches a product that nobody wants.</p>\n<p>Which conclusion best supports ERM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Financial hedging removes all enterprise risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Financial risks are the only significant risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Strong financial-risk management does not eliminate strategic/business risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The product failure is liquidity risk only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>This is one of the reasons risk management broadened beyond financial risk.</p></div></details></div></details>\n<details class=\"bank-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">Q35</span><div><p>Which statement best describes a \"holistic\" approach?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Every department manages risk independently</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> The firm manages only the largest financial risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Risks are considered together with strategy, governance and broader business functions</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> All risks are transferred to insurers</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong></div></details></div></details></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s50\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 18 - Questions based on wording traps</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>Lecturers love these.</p>\n<h4>\"Risk management is...\"</h4>\n<p>If you see:</p>\n<p>A. purely reactive<br />\nB. systematic<br />\nC. only insurance<br />\nD. only loss avoidance</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{B}$$</div></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>\"ERM primarily seeks to...\"</h4>\n<p>If you see:</p>\n<p>A. eliminate all risk<br />\nB. create long-term value through integrated risk management<br />\nC. transfer all risk<br />\nD. focus only on financial risks</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{B}$$</div></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>\"Silo approach means...\"</h4>\n<p>A. integrating risks<br />\nB. managing risks separately by function<br />\nC. eliminating departments<br />\nD. centralising every decision</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{B}$$</div></div></details>\n<hr />\n<h4>\"Traditional RM originally focused on...\"</h4>\n<p>A. ESG<br />\nB. AI risks<br />\nC. insurance/pure risks<br />\nD. strategic opportunities</p>\n<details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><p>Choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{C}$$</div></div></details></div></details><h2 class=\"notes-divider\" id=\"t2p-d51\">PART F - FINAL REVIEW</h2><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s52\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 19 - The evolution you should be able to reconstruct from memory</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-widget\" data-vis=\"t2_stage_quiz\"><div class=\"vis-title\">Which stage is this firm at?</div><div class=\"vis-root\"></div><figcaption>Interactive · Read what each firm does and name its stage of risk-management evolution.</figcaption></figure></div><p>Instead of memorising dates, memorise the <strong>problem each stage tried to solve</strong>:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Insurance</span></div>\n<p>Problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we finance pure losses?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Traditional RM</span></div>\n<p>Problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do departments manage specific physical and financial exposures?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Integrated RM</span></div>\n<p>Problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we connect risk with strategy and planning?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div>\n<p>Problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do we manage all material enterprise risks collectively to support long-term value?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>↓</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Holistic RM</span></div>\n<p>Problem:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>How do risk, governance, sustainability, compliance and broader business functions interact?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>That causal progression is far easier to remember than treating the history as random terminology.</p></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s53\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 20 - What I would memorise the night before</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><div class=\"notes-visual\"><span>Visual</span><figure class=\"vis-figure vis-sketch\" data-diagram=\"t2_concept_map\"><div class=\"sketch-slot\"></div><figcaption>Top row: the organisation and its risk. Bottom row: how managing that risk evolved.</figcaption></figure></div><p>These are the high-yield relationships:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sole Proprietorship → Unlimited liability\"><span>Sole Proprietorship</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Unlimited liability</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Sdn. Bhd./Corporation → Separate legal entity + limited shareholder liability\"><span>Sdn. Bhd./Corporation</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Separate legal entity + limited shareholder liability</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-flow boxed\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"LLP → Partnership flexibility + limited liability\"><span>LLP</span><i aria-hidden=\"true\">→</i><span>Partnership flexibility + limited liability</span></div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Strategic}\n=\n\\text{Where are we going?}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Tactical}\n=\n\\text{How will we get there?}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Operational}\n=\n\\text{Can we execute day-to-day?}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Compliance}\n=\n\\text{Are we following the rules?}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Pre-loss}\n=\n\\text{Prepare}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Post-loss}\n=\n\\text{Survive + Recover}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{Traditional RM}\n=\n\\text{insurance + physical/financial risk + silos}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{CRM}\n=\n\\text{credit + market + liquidity + hedging}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ERM}\n=\n\\text{holistic + integrated + enterprise-wide + value-based}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ERM objective}\n\\neq\n\\text{zero risk}\n}$$</div>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\boxed{\n\\text{ERM objective}\n=\n\\text{better decisions + long-term value}\n}$$</div></div></details><details class=\"note-section\" id=\"t2p-s54\"><summary><span class=\"ns-title\">Part 21 - The single most important MCQ trick for Topic 2</span><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"note-body\"><p>When two answers seem correct, ask:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Is the question describing the risk itself, the organisational level, or the management philosophy?</strong></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Example:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>The company enters China, finances the investment with foreign debt, and later its factory system crashes.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You potentially have:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Strategic risk}$$</div>\n<p>from entering China,</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Tactical/financial risk}$$</div>\n<p>from how it is financed,</p>\n<p>and:</p>\n<div class=\"math-display\">$$\\text{Operational risk}$$</div>\n<p>from the system failure.</p>\n<p>If the question asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which risk arises from the decision to enter China?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Strategic</span></div>\n<p>If it asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"Which risk arises from the IT breakdown?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>Operational</span></div>\n<p>If it asks:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"What approach considers all these risks together?\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>choose:</p>\n<div class=\"math-flow single boxed\"><span>ERM</span></div>\n<p>That separation between <strong>risk level, risk type and management approach</strong> is probably the biggest thing to master for Topic 2 MCQs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"question-bank-wrap hard-wrap\" id=\"t2-hard\"><summary><div><strong>Difficult mode - 30 questions</strong><small>18 Bloom-graded questions (3 per level) and 12 marked extension questions. Answers hidden.</small></div><span class=\"chevron\">⌄</span></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><p class=\"hard-intro\">Questions climb Bloom&#39;s taxonomy from <b>Remember</b> to <b>Create</b>. The <b>extension</b> questions go beyond the slides (industry practice, auditors, boards, Master&#39;s, PhD and postdoc level) but can be reasoned out from the topic&#39;s principles.</p><div class=\"action-row\"><button class=\"primary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"difficult\">Start Topic 2 difficult mode</button><button class=\"secondary-btn\" data-quiz=\"2\" data-quiz-mode=\"extensions\">Extensions only</button></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which list contains only the five Malaysian business forms named on the slides?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Sole proprietorship, partnership, cooperative, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sole proprietorship, partnership, LLP, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Partnership, LLC, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad, trust</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sole proprietorship, LLP, Sdn. Bhd., Berhad, government-linked company</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The slides list <strong>sole proprietorship, partnership, limited liability partnership (LLP), private limited company (Sdn. Bhd.) and public limited company (Berhad)</strong>. Cooperatives, trusts, LLCs (a US form) and GLCs are not in that list.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>Which is a <strong>post-loss</strong> objective of risk management?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Reduce anxiety</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Meet legal obligations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Prepare for potential losses in the most economical way</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Stabilise earnings</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Post-loss objectives: survival, continuity of operations, <strong>stable earnings</strong>, continued growth, and minimising effects on people and society. Reducing anxiety, meeting legal obligations and economical preparation are pre-loss objectives.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-1\">Remember</span></div><p>On the slides' enterprise-risk pyramid, at which level do credit, market, IT, marketing and competitive risk appear?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Strategic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Operational</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Tactical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Compliance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The slide lists these under <strong>tactical</strong> risk: risks that affect how the strategy is executed. Strategic risk concerns direction; operational risk daily execution; compliance risk legal permission.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>What does \"limited liability\" change for a Sdn. Bhd. shareholder?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It reduces the company's operational and market risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It caps the shareholder's exposure at roughly the amount invested, because the company is a separate legal entity</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It means the company cannot be sued</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It makes the shareholder responsible for management decisions</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Limited liability moves the boundary of <strong>who bears the loss</strong>; the company's own business risks are unchanged and it can still be sued. Shareholders do not manage the company by virtue of owning shares.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>Why did insurance-based risk management prove insufficient?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Insurance is too expensive for all firms</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Insurers refused to cover fire and flood</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Regulators banned insurance-based approaches</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Many important risks, such as poor strategy, reputation and changing customer behaviour, cannot simply be insured</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Insurance handles pure risks like fire and theft well, but uninsurable risks were neglected. You cannot claim from an insurer because management misread the market.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span></div><p>What is the main weakness of the silo approach?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each department manages its own risk, so correlated risks and dependencies across the enterprise can be missed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Specialists are less skilled than generalists</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It manages too many risks at once</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It ignores financial risks</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Silo reports can each be accurate yet miss that several risks depend on the same driver. Total enterprise risk ≠ the sum of isolated conclusions. Silos actually concentrated on financial and physical risks.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>After a flood, a manufacturer restarts production at a backup site within a week and keeps paying staff. Which objectives is it pursuing?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Pre-loss: reduce anxiety and meet legal obligations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Pre-loss: economical preparation</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Post-loss: survival and continuity of operations</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Neither; this is loss prevention</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>The loss has happened, so these are <strong>post-loss</strong> objectives: survive and continue operating (and stabilise earnings). Prevention would have acted before the flood.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>Three accountants want to share management while protecting each partner from the others' professional mistakes. Which structure fits best?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> LLP</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> General partnership</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Sole proprietorship</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Berhad</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An LLP combines partnership flexibility with company-like protection: partners are generally liable only to the extent of their investment. A general partnership exposes partners to joint and several liability; a Berhad is a listed company.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span></div><p>A bank decides to exit retail lending and focus on wealth management. Its accounting system later fails during the transition. Classify the two risks.</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Both strategic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Both operational</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Strategic and tactical</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Strategic (the change of direction) and operational (the system failure)</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Changing the business model is a <strong>strategic</strong> decision; a system failure in daily execution is <strong>operational</strong>. Keep decision direction separate from execution failure.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Four departments each assess their risk as \"low\", but all depend on stable China-US trade. Why can the enterprise still be in danger?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because departmental analysts are usually wrong</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because a common driver makes the risks correlated, so a single shock can trigger several losses at once</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because low risks are always underestimated</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because ERM requires every risk to be rated high</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The failure is not bad departmental analysis but <strong>dependency</strong>: correlated risks materialise together. ERM aggregates risks to see that combined exposure.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>A manager cuts maintenance and quality checks to hit this year's profit target. What does Topic 2 say about this?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> It maximises shareholder wealth because profit rises</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> It has no risk implications</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Short-term profit may rise while operational, quality and reputation risk increase, so it can reduce long-term shareholder wealth</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It is a compliance issue only</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Objectives can conflict: cost minimisation can raise operational risk. Shareholder wealth is long-term value, not this year's profit, which is why risk management cannot be separated from strategy.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span></div><p>Why does separating ownership from control in a corporation create new risks even as it solves others?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because shareholders become personally liable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because corporations cannot raise capital</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because management always acts against shareholders</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because managers may pursue goals (e.g. short-term bonuses) that differ from owners' long-term interests, creating agency and governance risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Limited liability and professional management solve concentration problems, but owners and managers can have different objectives, plus information asymmetry. \"Always\" overstates it; shareholders are not personally liable.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D13</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A rapidly expanding manufacturer asks whether to keep traditional RM or adopt ERM. Which answer is strongest?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Always adopt ERM because it is holistic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Keep traditional RM because it is cheaper</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Traditional RM may suit narrow, well-defined exposures, but growth creates dependencies and strategic complexity that make ERM more suitable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Adopt neither; buy more insurance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Evaluate-level answers give conditions and a mechanism, not slogans. As interconnections grow, silo management misses aggregated exposure, which is what ERM addresses.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D14</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>Project A offers 25% expected return with significant risk; Project B offers 5% with low risk. What should risk management conclude?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Choose B because it is safer</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Evaluate whether A's risk is understood, within capacity and appetite, controllable and compensated by its return before deciding</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Choose A because higher return is always better</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Reject both because both carry risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Risk management improves decisions under uncertainty; it does not automatically pick the safest option. Choosing B by reflex is avoidance disguised as expertise.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D15</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span></div><p>A debt-funded acquisition carries IT, single-supplier and environmental risks. The board asks: abandon it? Best response?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes, because there are many risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No, because expected market share is high</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Only if insurance is unavailable</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Not necessarily: treat the major risks, then compare the aggregated residual risk with the expected strategic return</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>\"Many risks\" is not a decision rule. ERM maps how the risks interact (supplier failure → production stop → revenue fall → debt pressure) and decides on residual risk versus value.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D16</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which design best reflects ERM for a multinational entering a new market?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Each department writes its own risk register; the board sees totals once a year</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Objectives → identification → strategic/tactical/operational/compliance assessment → risk owners → cross-risk aggregation → treatment → monitoring and reporting → board review</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Buy insurance for all assets, then proceed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Let the risk department own every risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>ERM links objectives to all levels of risk, assigns owners, aggregates across risks and reports to the board. The other options are silo, insurance-only or ownership-confusion designs.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D17</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>Which statement best captures the chapter's evolutionary logic in one sentence?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Risk management moved from reactive insurance of individual losses, through departmental and financial RM, to integrated enterprise-wide management of interconnected risks for long-term value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Risk management moved from ERM back to insurance as markets matured</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Risk management has always been holistic</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Risk management evolved to eliminate all risk</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>Each stage arose because the previous one became insufficient as organisations grew larger and more interconnected; ERM aims at long-term value, not zero risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">D18</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span></div><p>How should the roles of risk owners and the risk function be designed?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The risk function owns and manages every risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Business units ignore risk because the risk function handles it</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Business units own and manage the risks they generate; the risk function provides framework, measurement, challenge and reporting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The board manages all operational risks directly</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Operations understands machinery, treasury understands cash. Otherwise everyone takes risks and hands the consequences to the risk department.</p></div></details></div></details></div><h4 class=\"hard-level\"><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension questions</span><small>beyond the notes, lowest Bloom level first</small></h4><div class=\"question-bank\"><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X7</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-2\">Understand</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>In portfolio terms, why can several \"acceptable\" risks produce an unacceptable total?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Because variances never add</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Because expected values change when you add risks</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Because insurance premiums rise</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Because positive correlation raises the probability of joint losses, so the combined distribution has a fatter tail than independent risks would</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>With correlated risks, P(several losses together) rises sharply; independence assumptions understate tail exposure. This is the quantitative idea behind ERM aggregation.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X1</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-3\">Apply</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>A Malaysian SME bakery is run by one owner who personally guarantees a bank loan. If the bakery fails, what is the owner's exposure?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Personal assets may be used to repay the business debt</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only the bakery's equipment</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Nothing, because banks bear business risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Only the share capital</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>A sole proprietor has unlimited liability, and a personal guarantee makes the exposure explicit: business risk becomes personal risk.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X2</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Industry</span></div><p>An airline launches a new regional route network. Which item is a <strong>tactical</strong> rather than strategic risk?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Whether the new region will be profitable at all</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Whether to change the business model to long-haul</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Fuel hedging and aircraft financing for the launch</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Whether to exit the region entirely</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Financing and hedging are about <strong>executing</strong> the chosen strategy. Profitability of the region, business-model changes and exit decisions are strategic direction.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X3</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Auditor</span></div><p>An internal auditor finds that credit, liquidity and market risk are each reported by a different team using different scales, with no combined view. What is the main finding?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The firm is fully compliant, so no issue exists</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> A silo approach: the board cannot see aggregated, correlated exposure</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Too much risk reporting</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> The teams should be merged into IT</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>Different scales and no aggregation is the classic conventional-CRM silo problem the slides describe.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X8</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Master's</span></div><p>Why might holistic RM integrate sustainability rather than leave it to a CSR team?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Climate change, for example, drives physical, supply-chain, market, regulatory, financial, reputational and strategic risks at once</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Sustainability is legally required to be in the risk department</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> CSR teams are too expensive</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Sustainability risks are always small</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: A</strong><p>An environmental driver becomes multiple enterprise risks. Managing it separately recreates the silo problem.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X9</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-4\">Analyze</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Which statement about technology in modern risk management is most accurate?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Technology only reduces risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Technology only creates risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Technology is irrelevant to ERM</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Analytics and AI improve identification, modelling and real-time monitoring, but also create model, cyber, data-quality and dependency risks</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>A control can itself become a source of risk. The slides note technology's role in risk modelling, scenario analysis and monitoring; the double effect is the higher-order insight.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X4</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Auditor</span></div><p>A company's risk register was completed in January and has not been updated since. The auditor should conclude:</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> The process is complete</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Only insurance coverage matters</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The register is too detailed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Risk management is a cycle; without monitoring and review the register no longer reflects current risks</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Monitoring feeds back into identification. A static register is documentation, not risk management.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X5</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Board</span></div><p>A board proposes a policy to \"eliminate all risk from operations\". The best board-level critique?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Correct: ERM aims to eliminate risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Eliminating all risk would also eliminate investment, innovation and growth; the aim is appropriate risk for appropriate expected value</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> It should eliminate only reputational risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> It should transfer all risk to insurers instead</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>No risk ≠ optimal management. ERM optimises risk against value; total transfer is also neither possible nor desirable.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X10</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · PhD</span></div><p>Does the chapter's stage model imply older risks disappear as RM evolves?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Yes: insurance risk disappeared once ERM arrived</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> No: risk categories accumulated, so modern firms still face fire risk plus cyber, AI/model, ESG, geopolitical and supply-chain risk</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Yes: only strategic risk remains</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> No: but only financial risks remain</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: B</strong><p>The sequence insurance → market/financial → operational → strategic shows an expanding risk universe, not replacement.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X11</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-5\">Evaluate</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Critically, what is the strongest justification for ERM over traditional RM?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> ERM manages more risk types</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> ERM uses more spreadsheets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> The effect of one risk cannot always be understood independently of other risks or of corporate strategy</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> ERM removes the need for governance</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>\"More risk types\" is true but shallow. The deep justification is interaction and strategy-dependence, which require aggregation against objectives.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X6</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Board</span></div><p>Which board agenda item best shows <strong>integrated</strong> (not traditional) risk management for a proposed acquisition?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Approve the deal, then ask the risk team to insure the target's assets</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Ask finance alone to hedge the currency</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Review strategic, FX, regulatory, supply-chain and reputational risks together before deciding whether to proceed</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> Delegate the decision entirely to the CFO</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: C</strong><p>Integrated RM brings risk into the strategic decision itself, across categories, rather than cleaning up afterwards.</p></div></details></div></details><details class=\"bank-question hard-question\"><summary><span class=\"qno\">X12</span><div><div class=\"bloom-badges\"><span class=\"bloom-chip bloom-6\">Create</span><span class=\"ext-badge\">Extension · Postdoc</span></div><p>Design a governance-light way for a mid-sized firm to capture correlation without a full ERM department. Which is most consistent with Topic 2?</p></div></summary><div class=\"bank-body\"><div class=\"bank-options\"><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>A.</b> Keep separate registers but add no common scale</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>B.</b> Outsource all risks to an insurer</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>C.</b> Rate every risk \"high\" to be safe</div><div class=\"bank-option\"><b>D.</b> A common risk scale and register, a quarterly cross-functional review that maps shared drivers (e.g. one market, one supplier), and aggregated reporting to the board</div></div><details class=\"answer-reveal\"><summary>Reveal answer and logic</summary><div class=\"answer-box\"><strong>Answer: D</strong><p>Shared scales, explicit dependency mapping and aggregation address the silo weakness proportionately; the other options either keep silos, transfer blindly or destroy information.</p></div></details></div></details></div></div></details>");
